1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:16,190
 You told us about the Spanish, well, the French, didn't they discover anything?

2
00:00:16,590 --> 00:00:19,290
 Yes, France in those days was really a great power.

3
00:00:19,450 --> 00:00:22,210
 Oh, yes, but King Francois I had quite a few troubles.

4
00:00:22,590 --> 00:00:26,730
 Fact of the matter is, he had already commissioned one of the great navigators of the time,

5
00:00:26,830 --> 00:00:32,690
 the Florentine, Giovanni de Verrazano, to discover a passage to that new land, Cathay, that we call China.

6
00:00:33,180 --> 00:00:36,360
 Well, he was a humanist and a scientist and a man of rare intelligence.

7
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:43,550
 In January 1524, with his ship the Dauphine, he set sail and reached the continent of North America,

8
00:00:43,670 --> 00:00:50,270
 named for another Italian, Amerigo Vespucci, who had set foot several thousand kilometers away 20 years earlier in Brazil.

9
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:54,680
 That's how history's made, my children, and geography, too.

10
00:00:54,740 --> 00:00:56,180
 But let's get back to Verrazano.

11
00:00:56,780 --> 00:01:00,120
 Here he is in sight of the coast, the coast of Carolina.

12
00:01:01,880 --> 00:01:07,160
 I feel sure this land is inhabited.

13
00:01:07,380 --> 00:01:09,280
 Take eight men and reconnoiter the area.

14
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:12,790
 On your guard, but I don't want any hostile acts against the natives.

15
00:01:14,310 --> 00:01:15,990
 Aye, aye, sir. We'll carry out the orders.

16
00:01:16,790 --> 00:01:17,780
 Give them gifts.

17
00:01:19,830 --> 00:01:22,180
 Sure, you follow my orders strictly.

18
00:01:22,650 --> 00:01:24,100
 You should be friendly and calm.

19
00:01:40,620 --> 00:03:45,300
 And then to Cape Breton Islands, before returning to Europe.

20
00:03:47,380 --> 00:03:50,810
 He was the first to say that the New World was a new continent as well.

21
00:03:51,090 --> 00:03:54,600
 There was no passage to the West, and he was the first man who dared to say it.

22
00:03:55,170 --> 00:03:59,280
 He said the Earth's globe was much more vast than ancient philosophers believed.

23
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:05,010
 no doubt about it verrazano was a great man the indians they liked him a lot too this verrazano

24
00:04:05,010 --> 00:04:18,960
 yes more than you might suppose during a voyage in 1528 they ended up by eating him francois the

25
00:04:18,960 --> 00:04:23,440
 first never bothered to look at the many reports made by verrazano the king had a few other troubles

26
00:04:23,440 --> 00:04:28,780
 huh could he have troubles when somebody's a king things are easy no no they're not he was lumbered

27
00:04:28,780 --> 00:04:34,350
 with a war that dragged on interminably with spain's charles v and to cap it all the constable

28
00:04:34,350 --> 00:04:38,490
 of bourbon the chief of the french armies had just gone over to the enemy

29
00:04:39,080 --> 00:04:40,470
 He had to fight on and on.

30
00:04:40,650 --> 00:04:43,750
 There was victory and defeat and all is lost but honor.

31
00:04:44,160 --> 00:04:45,040
 Well, you know how it is.

32
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:52,460
 In 1533, Pizarro was showering Francois I's rival, Charles V, with Peruvian gold.

33
00:04:52,460 --> 00:04:58,980
 So the French king thought that gold from the new continent would give him a way of bringing victory to France.

34
00:04:59,550 --> 00:05:05,330
 And it would also help to restore his treasury, which was under considerable strain with all his royal residences,

35
00:05:05,650 --> 00:05:08,540
 especially Chambord, which was being built.

36
00:05:14,260 --> 00:05:21,150
 What do you think I pay you for?

37
00:05:21,150 --> 00:06:00,000
 For Jacques has just been commissioned by Francois I to resume his explorations beyond Newfoundland.

38
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,490
 Cartier is only 30 and the darling of the port city of Saint-Malo,

39
00:06:03,490 --> 00:06:07,200
 but he's an experienced navigator with a good knowledge of the American coast.

40
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:11,100
 Captain Cartier, I welcome you to my humble inn.

41
00:06:11,100 --> 00:06:18,720
 The handsomest of men, sir.

42
00:06:18,720 --> 00:06:32,590
 Francois I, I am recruiting able-bodied men for an expedition beyond Newfoundland.

43
00:06:32,590 --> 00:06:37,280
 He's gonna steal our fishermen. We won't have crews.

44
00:06:37,280 --> 00:06:40,000
 Yes, and then how are we going to make any profit?

45
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,470
 Won't let him do it.

46
00:06:41,470 --> 00:06:50,300
 You want us to be devouring by cannibals like Verrazano? Is that what you want?

47
00:06:50,300 --> 00:06:55,870
 Oh? What man you want, Captain Cartier?

48
00:06:55,870 --> 00:07:06,290
 Fishermen have both have contracts to fish for me, so they can't go with you and be cannibal food.

49
00:07:06,290 --> 00:07:11,050
 You run along and look someplace else.

50
00:07:11,050 --> 00:07:39,310
 Jay was having a hard time recruiting his crew. He needed a royal decree.

51
00:07:39,310 --> 00:07:44,550
 Citizens, we, Francois I, King of France, command:

52
00:07:44,550 --> 00:07:48,990
 No ship shall leave this port until Captain Cartier has sailed with a full crew.

53
00:07:48,990 --> 00:07:59,360
 Maybe I've got just the men you might need.

54
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:03,290
 I'm going to make sure they're on your ship. You'll see, Captain, I'll bring them here.

55
00:08:03,290 --> 00:08:14,010
 Rich treasure, right? Let's go!

56
00:08:14,010 --> 00:08:22,530
 On the 20th of April, 1534, two small ships set sail from San Malo with 61 hands on board.

57
00:08:22,530 --> 00:08:27,330
 In only 21 days, they were in sight of Newfoundland, and then they sailed north.

58
00:08:27,330 --> 00:08:37,890
 I'd rather turn this boat around and go home. You've gotten me into a fine mess, thanks!

59
00:08:37,890 --> 00:08:48,380
 This will be St. Severin Harbor. That, the St. Jacques River.

60
00:08:48,380 --> 00:08:50,580
 Look, look, over there!

61
00:08:53,180 --> 00:09:01,660
 Friends are venturing into uncharted waters sailing into the vast unknown.

62
00:09:01,660 --> 00:09:03,960
 Regions appearing on nomads.

63
00:09:03,960 --> 00:09:11,290
 This will be the island of Brion and the island of Margo.

64
00:09:11,290 --> 00:09:33,910
 Overgrown.

65
00:09:33,910 --> 00:09:47,020
 We shall return.

66
00:09:47,020 --> 00:09:53,070
 It could be dangerous here.

67
00:09:53,070 --> 00:09:58,750
 Dama na swatu.

68
00:09:58,750 --> 00:10:33,010
 July 34 marked the beginning.

69
00:10:33,010 --> 00:10:38,160
 The Canadian fur trade will have a brilliant future and helped to shape Canadian history.

70
00:10:38,160 --> 00:10:42,360
 The Indians are being taken for a ride selling these beautiful furs here for worthless junk.

71
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:43,390
 No, that's just not right.

72
00:10:43,390 --> 00:10:49,310
 You're right. Yes, but all of these quartz crystals, these baubles and shells, and especially the copper,

73
00:10:49,430 --> 00:10:52,250
 all that meant to their minds an idea of rebirth after death.

74
00:10:52,500 --> 00:10:54,580
 Soon they began to want other items, yes.

75
00:10:54,820 --> 00:10:56,600
 Pretty soon it was axes and knives.

76
00:10:56,880 --> 00:10:58,940
 They were quick to see how useful they could be.

77
00:11:39,830 --> 00:11:44,890
 Don Acona wasn't pleased to have these foreigners come and live on his lands, and he said so.

78
00:11:45,210 --> 00:11:48,510
 I have an idea. I invite them to come aboard. We can explain things.

79
00:11:48,510 --> 00:11:58,070
 He was to persuade Chief Don Acona to let his two sons go to France, and Cartier promised to bring them back soon.

80
00:12:01,290 --> 00:12:08,400
 On the return journey, Coutier would twice miss the St. Lawrence estuary.

81
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:10,500
 Never mind, he would succeed next time.

82
00:12:10,500 --> 00:12:18,860
 To the St. Lawrence, it was there.

83
00:12:18,860 --> 00:12:21,860
 Yes, and why did he zig-zag so much on the way? Explain that.

84
00:12:21,860 --> 00:12:25,660
 Why did he want to bring the Indian boys along with him?

85
00:12:25,660 --> 00:12:29,010
 And tell me why the boys look like me. I'd like to know.

86
00:12:29,010 --> 00:12:33,010
 Well, now, why did he miss the St. Lawrence? Because it was the wrong season.

87
00:12:33,010 --> 00:12:36,010
 The currents were bad, the winds blowing in the wrong direction,

88
00:12:36,010 --> 00:12:39,930
 and besides, his crew had had enough and wanted to go home.

89
00:12:39,930 --> 00:12:49,720
 Today, we find it difficult to imagine, in any real sense, their comprehension of the world, then.

90
00:12:49,920 --> 00:12:52,360
 Let me show you something. It's interesting.

91
00:12:52,970 --> 00:12:57,510
 This is the best, the most detailed map at the time, and look at the coast of America.

92
00:12:57,750 --> 00:13:02,010
 An outline, and behind it, nothing, the unknown. You understand?

93
00:13:02,800 --> 00:13:04,920
 Now, let me show you another one.

94
00:13:05,630 --> 00:13:06,330
 Look here.

95
00:13:06,690 --> 00:13:13,550
 Twenty years later, in 1550, you see here, the western coast is drawn in, and what do we see?

96
00:13:13,850 --> 00:13:20,580
 Japan is here, China is here, India is there, and Florida, look at Florida, in the north, it's all nonsense.

97
00:13:20,860 --> 00:13:23,160
 And that's what they thought the world was like.

98
00:13:23,540 --> 00:13:27,060
 And that's why those earliest explorers were so very important.

99
00:13:27,060 --> 00:13:36,930
 They were making the acquaintance of our people, the two Indian boys, well, he was to return to America with them, 1535.

100
00:13:37,730 --> 00:13:44,330
 Now, during their stay in France, they learned French, and Cartier was counting on their help in his future expeditions.

101
00:13:44,720 --> 00:13:50,590
 This time he had the Grand Hermine with its 12 cannons, the Petite Hermine and the Aimerion.

102
00:13:50,590 --> 00:13:55,450
 112 officers and men, and a writer on board who's not exactly an unknown.

103
00:13:55,450 --> 00:13:58,650
 It's much easier this way.

104
00:13:58,650 --> 00:14:02,140
 And just what are you noting down?

105
00:14:02,140 --> 00:14:04,680
 I'm telling about our trip.

106
00:14:04,680 --> 00:14:11,840
 Today, the 13th day of August, 1535, we have entered a bay which our captain has named the Bay of St. Lawrence.

107
00:14:11,840 --> 00:14:13,790
 Oh, up the bow, look, look!

108
00:14:13,790 --> 00:14:36,360
 So beautiful.

109
00:14:36,360 --> 00:14:41,760
 So very beautiful.

110
00:14:41,760 --> 00:14:50,780
 River narrows, you will find Saguenay River, then Canada.

111
00:14:50,780 --> 00:14:52,120
 Farther up, Hochelaga.

112
00:14:52,360 --> 00:14:53,850
 There you find gold, you see.

113
00:14:54,400 --> 00:14:56,100
 And the passage to China, to the ocean.

114
00:14:56,240 --> 00:14:56,840
 There is none.

115
00:14:57,910 --> 00:14:58,090
 No.

116
00:15:01,110 --> 00:15:08,760
 I've been going back and forth looking for the passage, that elusive passage.

117
00:15:08,880 --> 00:15:11,900
 But the boys are right, the passage is a path that exists.

118
00:15:12,270 --> 00:15:12,760
 Up there?

119
00:15:17,540 --> 00:15:26,440
 We are in Canada, the land of our father.

120
00:15:28,840 --> 00:15:33,260
 Tadakome, one day it will be called Quebec, oh yes, and then so be it.

121
00:16:00,650 --> 00:16:20,560
 Why do you want to leave?

122
00:16:20,870 --> 00:16:22,630
 Why do you want to go up to the Hochelaga?

123
00:16:22,890 --> 00:16:24,670
 Stay here with us, we are your friends.

124
00:16:24,850 --> 00:16:26,050
 There's only ice and snow.

125
00:16:26,450 --> 00:16:28,470
 There are evil people up there, you will die.

126
00:16:28,470 --> 00:16:32,710
 There is no need for concern. Our lord will watch over us. Will you come, too?

127
00:16:32,970 --> 00:16:33,330
 No.

128
00:16:34,680 --> 00:16:37,790
 Said you would, and I would reward you if you come.

129
00:16:38,130 --> 00:16:38,430
 No.

130
00:16:38,760 --> 00:16:39,020
 No.

131
00:16:39,620 --> 00:16:42,620
 Let's go aboard the ship. We'll be able to discuss it.

132
00:16:45,020 --> 00:16:45,800
 No.

133
00:16:46,420 --> 00:16:51,640
 Doubt about it. Jacques Cartier doesn't know about Indians. They're afraid of competition in their trading.

134
00:16:52,340 --> 00:16:57,570
 They have learned not to trust him. Naturally, he doesn't know how to reassure them.

135
00:17:16,560 --> 00:18:10,910
 Tanker, look alive. What I call a reception.

136
00:18:13,690 --> 00:18:14,350
 Bear the name.

137
00:18:16,200 --> 00:19:51,290
 The exploration of the St. Lawrence River will go no farther than the village of Hachalanga, that is, Montreal.

138
00:19:52,030 --> 00:19:57,250
 It's already mid-October, and Cartier returns to his companions who stayed in Stadocone, Quebec.

139
00:19:57,970 --> 00:19:59,810
 Their behavior wasn't always commendable.

140
00:20:16,440 --> 00:20:29,260
 Before winter, fire sticks too much. Your men are bad with many of our people, our subjects.

141
00:20:38,270 --> 00:21:01,250
 Waiting for it, a stand. Surprised.

142
00:21:27,650 --> 00:21:50,110
 Winter, curvy and there's no known cure.

143
00:21:50,110 --> 00:21:55,260
 Better go and see the Indians. Their medicine man must make medicine for it. We'll go ask him.

144
00:21:56,670 --> 00:22:04,080
 Motions made with a white cedar they say is the remedy and they were quite right

145
00:22:04,080 --> 00:22:09,420
 It's a splendid cure with plenty of vitamin C. It is a new remedy. Who wants to try it?

146
00:22:09,420 --> 00:22:49,410
 Who will try it out of our cap? Good for you lad now you drink and we all wanted to go home to France

147
00:22:49,410 --> 00:22:53,810
 But where was the crew we have suffered the misfortune to lose 25 men?

148
00:22:53,810 --> 00:22:59,030
 We have no choice but to abandon one ship. I'm sorry. Abandon one of our ships. We haven't the men for a crew

149
00:22:59,030 --> 00:23:43,080
 We must take gifts to the king to impress him chiefs will come back home

150
00:23:43,080 --> 00:23:47,780
 there will be a third voyage a few years later under the command of a doubtful character named

151
00:23:47,780 --> 00:23:54,570
 Oberwald. But as for the gold and diamonds that Cartier brought back, they were quartz and iron

152
00:23:54,570 --> 00:23:57,850
 pyrites, the sort of worthless treasure that we gave the Indians.

